TITLE:: PltBifurcation
summary:: Bifurcation diagram of a one dimensional map
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltScatter, Classes/PltPhase

DESCRIPTION::
Sweep a parameter along x and plot the attractor vertically: one fixed point
becomes two, then four, then chaos.

Each column runs the orbit from code::x0::, throws away code::transient::
iterations so only the attractor is left, then records code::keep:: of them.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: mapFunc
Takes the current value and the swept parameter, returns the next value:
code::{ |x, r| r * x * (1 - x) }::
argument:: paramLo
argument:: paramHi
argument:: x0
argument:: columns
How many parameter values across the sweep.
argument:: transient
Iterations discarded per column.
argument:: keep
Iterations plotted per column.
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: points
The computation on its own, with no window. Values that are NaN or larger than 1e6
are dropped, so a divergent map contributes nothing rather than infinities.
returns:: code::[params, values]::, two FloatArrays with one entry per plotted
point. FloatArray is single precision, so do not compare the parameters at 1e-9.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: zoom
Recompute over a narrower parameter window, keeping the same map. The interesting
structure is self-similar, so this is the main way to use the class.
argument:: newLo
argument:: newHi
argument:: columns
argument:: transient
argument:: keep

METHOD:: mapFunc
METHOD:: paramLo
METHOD:: paramHi
METHOD:: x0

EXAMPLES::
code::
// the logistic map, with a marker at one parameter value
p = PltBifurcation({ |x, r| r * x * (1 - x) }, 2.4, 4.0, 0.5).marker_(3.82).front;

// into the first period-doubling window
p.zoom(3.53, 3.6);
p.zoom(2.4, 4.0);

// the tent map
(
PltBifurcation({ |x, r| if(x <= 0.5, { 2 * r * x }, { 2 * r * (1 - x) }) },
	0.4, 1.0, 0.25, 460, 200, 90, "tent map").marker_(0.92).front;
)

// the sine map
(
PltBifurcation({ |x, r| r * sin(pi * x) }, 0.5, 1.0, 0.5, 460, 300, 100,
	"sine map").front;
)

// data only: the period at a given parameter value
(
PltBifurcation.points({ |x, r| r * x * (1 - x) }, 3.5, 3.5, 0.5, 1, 2000, 64)
	[1].collect(_.round(1e-5)).asSet.size;   // 4
)
::
